{% schema %}
  {
    
    "name": "Section 2",
    "tag": "section",
    "class": "gps-636010617391612582 gps gpsil",
    "settings": [{"type":"paragraph","content":"Section design by GemPages. [Click here to start design](https://admin.shopify.com/store/photobrickusa/apps/gempages-cro/app/shopify/edit?pageType=GP_ARTICLE&editorId=636010617324438182&sectionId=636010617391612582)"},{"type":"select","label":"Section Preload","id":"section_preload","options":[{"label":"On","value":"true"},{"label":"Off","value":"false"},{"label":"Off Lazy Script","value":"off_lazy_script"}],"default":"false"},{"type":"text","label":"Checksum","id":"checksum"},{"type":"html","id":"ggJm6-9N8lO_text","label":"ggJm6-9N8lO_text","default":"Choose Something Everyone Can Enjoy"},{"type":"html","id":"ggLnGGDLgV5_text","label":"ggLnGGDLgV5_text","default":"<p>The image you choose for the mosaic can make the activity even more engaging. Instead of selecting something that only one person cares about, choose an image that gives everyone a reason to get involved. It could be a family picture, a funny pet photo, a favorite holiday scene, a travel photograph, a beloved car or motorcycle, or even an image that represents something the whole family enjoys. You can make choosing the image part of the gathering too. Let everyone suggest their favorite options and decide together which one should become the next project.</p>"},{"type":"html","id":"ggPIgGYMHFw_srcSet_desktop_src","label":"ggPIgGYMHFw_srcSet_desktop_src","default":"https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0375/0107/5595/files/4eef3c49-5d5e-4569-8b47-e79b1efc5a20.png"},{"type":"html","id":"ggCHNzFX81s_text","label":"ggCHNzFX81s_text","default":"Building Together Creates Natural Conversation"},{"type":"html","id":"ggs5cpA7vE3_text","label":"ggs5cpA7vE3_text","default":"<p>One of the best things about a shared activity is that it does not require everyone to focus on the same thing every second. People can build while talking, joke about someone choosing the wrong piece, help each other find a missing color, and take breaks whenever they want. The activity becomes part of the conversation rather than something that interrupts it. This can be especially useful when relatives or friends who do not see each other often are gathered in one place. A simple project gives people something to do while making conversation feel more natural.</p>"},{"type":"html","id":"ggwLw9iC0af_text","label":"ggwLw9iC0af_text","default":"Make It a Project for All Ages"},{"type":"html","id":"ggLoDjoAwJd_text","label":"ggLoDjoAwJd_text","default":"<p>Holiday gatherings often include several generations, which can make it difficult to find an activity that everyone enjoys. A brick mosaic offers different ways to participate. Younger children can help organize pieces, older children can follow sections of the design, adults can work on more detailed areas, and grandparents can contribute while enjoying the conversation. Everyone does not need to work at the same speed or complete the same task. The goal is simply to create something together.</p>"},{"type":"html","id":"ggtVH33jP7-_text","label":"ggtVH33jP7-_text","default":"Watch the Artwork Slowly Come to Life"},{"type":"html","id":"ggWX5kCdW0i_text","label":"ggWX5kCdW0i_text","default":"<p>The most rewarding part of building a mosaic is seeing the image develop. At the beginning, there may be nothing more than a collection of small colorful pieces. As the group continues, patterns begin to appear and recognizable details slowly emerge. Someone might notice a face first, while another person spots the outline of a vehicle or pet. These little discoveries give everyone something to look forward to as the project progresses. The finished image becomes the result of many small contributions rather than something that simply appears at the end.</p>"},{"type":"html","id":"ggq91id4Gpg_text","label":"ggq91id4Gpg_text","default":"You Do Not Have to Finish It That Night&nbsp;"},{"type":"html","id":"gg13Ac74FMh_text","label":"gg13Ac74FMh_text","default":"<p>A holiday project does not have to be completed before everyone goes home. In fact, taking your time can make the activity more enjoyable. You can build part of the mosaic after dinner, leave it organized on the table, and continue the next day or during the holiday break. Larger mosaics can become an ongoing project that gives the family something creative to return to. Instead of treating it like another activity that needs to be completed quickly, you can enjoy the process at your own pace.</p>"},{"type":"html","id":"ggUT0dY8oi2_text","label":"ggUT0dY8oi2_text","default":"<p>A holiday gathering does not have to end when dinner is finished. The hours afterward can be just as meaningful when everyone has something enjoyable to do together. A brick mosaic gives families and friends a simple way to put their phones aside, stay around the same table, and create something piece by piece.</p><p>More importantly, it gives the gathering another shared experience. Everyone can contribute, everyone can watch the image develop, and everyone can celebrate when the final piece is added.</p><p>This holiday season, clear the dinner table, bring everyone together, and build something you can enjoy long after the meal is over.</p><p><strong>Because the best holiday gatherings are not only about what you eat. They are about what you do together.</strong></p>"}],
    "blocks": [{"type": "@app"}],
    "locales": {}
  }
{% endschema %}